%I A402158 #6 Nov 22 2026 01:08:07
%S A402158 3,7,17,233,1373,1531,5861,27427,52937,125287
%N A402158 Primes p such that the iteration of T(z) = (z + 2)*(z^7 + 5*z^6 + 6*z^5 - 2*z^4 - 4*z^3 + 2*z^2 + 9*z - 1) + z collapses every element of GF(p) to -2.
%C A402158 P(z) = z^7 + 5*z^6 + 6*z^5 - 2*z^4 - 4*z^3 + 2*z^2 + 9*z - 1 has discriminant -2^10*251^3, P(-2) = -11, and T(z) - z = (z + 2)*P(z). The splitting field of P is the Hilbert class field H of Q(sqrt(-251)) (class number 7), with Gal(H/Q) = D_7.
%C A402158 If Kronecker(-251, p) = -1 (p != 2), Frobenius acts as a reflection, so P has exactly one root mod p; it is a fixed point of T different from -2 when p != 11. Hence every term p > 11 satisfies Kronecker(-251, p) = 1, and then -2 is the only fixed point iff p is represented by one of the non-principal forms 3*x^2 + x*y + 21*y^2 (p = 3, 1373, 52937), 7*x^2 + x*y + 9*y^2 (p = 7, 17, 1531, 5861) or 5*x^2 + 3*x*y + 13*y^2 (p = 233, 27427, 125287).
%C A402158 No other terms below 10^6 (exhaustive full functional-graph traversal of GF(p) for every prime p); a bounded-step orbit scan up to 5*10^8 found no further terms.
%H A402158 Igor M. Zhuk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23420627">Unital Collapse Primes in Arithmetic Dynamics: Total Backward-Orbit Collapse for T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 mod p, Galois Stratification, and Fermat-Totient Asymptotics</a>, Zenodo preprint (2026).
%H A402158 Igor M. Zhuk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23856837">Total-Collapse Primes of Polynomial Maps over GF(p): Eighteen Families and Two Related Sequences</a>, Zenodo preprint (2026); <a href="https://github.com/Nondual-Observer/total-collapse-primes-oeis">GitHub verification suite</a>.
%F A402158 p is in the sequence iff #{z in {0..p-1} : T^(p)(z) == p - 2 (mod p)} = p, where T(z) = (z + 2)*(z^7 + 5*z^6 + 6*z^5 - 2*z^4 - 4*z^3 + 2*z^2 + 9*z - 1) + z mod p and T^(k) denotes the k-th iterate of T.
%e A402158 For p = 3: -2 == 1 (mod 3), and every element of GF(3) reaches 1 within 2 steps under iteration of T(z) mod 3; thus 3 is in the sequence.
%e A402158 For p = 233: p = 5*4^2 + 3*4*3 + 13*3^2 = 233 in Cl(-251), P_251(z) is irreducible mod 233, and all 233 elements of GF(233) collapse to -2 == 231 (mod 233) within 27 steps; thus 233 is in the sequence.
%o A402158 (Julia)
%o A402158 using Primes
%o A402158 function isok(p::Int)
%o A402158     (!isprime(p) || p == 2 || (p > 17 && powermod(-251, p >> 1, p) != 1)) && return false
%o A402158     function f(c)
%o A402158         s = 1
%o A402158         for a in (5, 6, -2, -4, 2, 9, -1); s = mod(s*c + a, p); end
%o A402158         return mod((c + 2)*s + c, p)
%o A402158     end
%o A402158     st = zeros(UInt8, p); st[1] = st[mod(p - 2, p) + 1] = 2
%o A402158     for z in 1:p-1
%o A402158         st[z + 1] == 2 && continue
%o A402158         c = z; while st[c + 1] == 0; st[c + 1] = 1; c = f(c); end
%o A402158         st[c + 1] == 1 && return false
%o A402158         c = z; while st[c + 1] == 1; st[c + 1] = 2; c = f(c); end
%o A402158     end
%o A402158     return true
%o A402158 end
%o A402158 println([p for p in 3:130000 if isok(p)])
%Y A402158 Cf. A401173, A401667, A401959. See the linked article for the companion sequence for (z + 2)*(z^3 + 2*z - 1) + z.
%K A402158 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A402158 1,1
%A A402158 _Igor Zhuk_, Nov 13 2026
